---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import NameDrift from "../components/NameDrift.astro";
import Distribution from "../components/Distribution.astro";
import Census1945 from "../components/Census1945.astro";
import names from "../data/names.json";
import gn from "../data/givennames.json";
---
<Base title="The Name" eyebrow="Nine spellings, one family"
      dek="Every shift has a cause, and the cause is almost always a change of ruler, clerk or language. The name is the family's best surviving record of what happened to it.">

<section class="blk">
  <NameDrift />
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Why each form exists</h2>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th class="m">Era</th><th>Where</th><th>What produced it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{names.variants.map(v=>(
        <tr><td>{v.form}</td><td class="m">{v.era}</td><td>{v.where}</td><td>{v.why}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">Two political events force name changes rather than merely allowing them. Between 1918 and 1943 Italian authorities in Istria Italianised Slavic surnames wholesale — by translating the meaning where there was one, otherwise by clipping and re-suffixing. After 1945 the pressure ran the other way, and some families who changed back in 1956 kept the Italianised spelling anyway. A Defranceschi in Istria may therefore appear under a name that is not etymologically this name at all — and a family of entirely different origin may appear under yours.</div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the spelling does and does not show</h2>
  <p class="dek">An earlier draft of this page claimed the two spellings sorted cleanly along the border abolished in 1797. Checked settlement by settlement, they do not. Here is what the census actually supports.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:26px"><Census1945 /></div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:26px">
    <p>The <strong>fused</strong> form is used in every Defranceschi household of the Habsburg Pazin district — Gologorica, Gračišće, Pazin, Pićan. But it is also used at Vodnjan, Barban, Motovun, Vižinada and Čepić, all of which were <em>Venetian</em>: seventeen of the nineteen families in formerly Venetian territory write it as one word. The fused form is simply everywhere.</p><p>What is one-directional is the other spelling. The two <strong>spaced</strong> households — at Seghetto near Umag and at Svetvinčenat — are both on the Venetian side, and there is not one in the Habsburg interior. The spaced form survives only where Venice ruled. That is a real asymmetry, and a smaller claim than the one this page used to make.</p>
    <p>The fusion is still best explained as an Austrian administrative habit — it is the same habit behind the modern concentration of the fused spelling in Trentino, Austrian until 1918 and today home to 74 per cent of Italy's Defranceschi. But after 1815 the whole peninsula was Austrian, so the habit had a hundred years to reach the Venetian side too. And in all of Istria in 1945 there was not one household spelling it plainly <em>Franceschi</em>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Where the name is now</h2>
  <p class="dek">628 people carry the fused spelling worldwide, across fourteen countries.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:24px"><Distribution spelling="Defranceschi" /></div>
  <p class="cap">Within Italy: {names.distribution.sets[0].italy.map(i=>`${i[0]} ${i[1]}%`).join(" · ")}.</p>

  <h3 class="lab" style="margin-top:40px">And the spelling this archive's owner carries</h3>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:10px">Seventeen people worldwide spell it <em>Defranceski</em> — and thirteen of them are in South Africa, which is one of the three destinations the Gologorica branch is recorded as reaching.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:22px"><Distribution spelling="Defranceski" /></div>

  <h3 class="lab" style="margin-top:40px">The parent name, for scale</h3>
  <div style="margin-top:22px"><Distribution spelling="Franceschi" /></div>
  <p class="cap">Franceschi without the prefix is a different order of magnitude and a different history — heavily Corsican and Caribbean-Latin American. Shared spelling is not shared descent. Source: Forebears.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Three Croatian clusters, three fates</h2>
  <p class="dek">The same patronymic settles in three parts of Croatia under three spellings — and only one of them is surviving.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Spelling</th><th>Where it belongs</th><th>Where they live now</th><th class="m">Today</th><th class="m">Mid-century</th><th>Trend</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{names.croatia_clusters.rows.map(r=>(
        <tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td>{r[1]}</td><td>{r[2]}</td><td class="m">{r[3]}</td><td class="m">{r[4]}</td>
        <td style={r[5]==="growing"?"color:var(--accent)":"color:var(--terra)"}>{r[5]}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:26px">
    <p>The Istrian <strong>Defranceschi</strong> have fallen from about fifty to about ten. The Rijeka <strong>Franceski</strong> — thirty at mid-century, in Rijeka and at Donja Banda on Pelješac — are down to a single household. The Dalmatian <strong>Franceschi</strong> of Split and Imotski have gone the other way, from fifty to sixty, in thirty households.</p>
    <p>And the Dalmatian spelling has a cause no other form on this page shares. Their <em>de</em> was not dropped by a clerk or worn away by emigration. It was <strong>abolished by decree of the Napoleonic administration</strong>, which stripped nobiliary particles — so the descendants of a count who stormed Imotski in 1717 are plain Franceschi today.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Every spelling, counted</h2>
  <p class="dek">Thirteen recorded forms worldwide. The tail is long and thin — several spellings have a single bearer alive.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Spelling</th><th class="m">Bearers</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{names.spellings_world.rows.map(r=>(
        <tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td class="m">{r[1].toLocaleString()}</td><td>{r[2]}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap">Source: {names.spellings_world.source}. {names.spellings_world.note} Franceschi is approximate — it runs to five figures across more than forty-six countries and is not usefully counted alongside the rest.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>And the given names move too</h2>
  <p class="dek">The surname is only half of it. The same man is Joannes in the register, Giovanni in the town, Zuane on the Venetian coast, Ivan to his neighbours, Johann to the Austrian clerk and John when he lands in New York. A living member of this family can be Ivan, Giovanni or John depending on who is writing.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Latin, in the register</th><th>Italian</th><th>Venetian</th><th>Croatian</th><th>German</th><th>English</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{gn.rows.map(r=>(
        <tr><td>{r.latin}</td><td>{r.italian}</td><td>{r.venetian}</td><td>{r.croatian}</td><td>{r.german}</td><td>{r.english}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:26px">
    <p>This is not a curiosity — it decides whether a family can be reconstructed at all. Grouping records by parents only works if the machine knows that <em>Valentini</em>, <em>Valentino</em> and <em>Valentinus</em> are one man, and that <em>Ursula</em>, <em>Ursulæ</em> and <em>Orsola</em> are one woman. The normaliser behind the household pages now carries <strong>{gn.names} canonical names across {gn.forms} recorded forms</strong>, in Latin, Italian, Venetian, Croatian, German and English, and it peels Latin case endings and drops the <em>de</em> of a surname before comparing.</p>
    <p>Adding the Slavic and English forms alone merged three separate Rijeka households of the 1620s into one — <strong>Gioannis Antonÿ De Franceschi and Lutia</strong>, six children between 1626 and 1632 — which had been split three ways purely because the index wrote his name differently on different pages.</p>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
